---
import ExerciseLayout from '../layouts/ExerciseLayout.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
---
<ExerciseLayout
  title="Bench Press One Rep Max Calculator — Estimate Your Bench 1RM"
  description="Estimate your bench press one rep max from any set of 1–12 reps. Six formulas, the range between them, training percentages and plate loading."
  path="/bench-press-calculator/"
  name="Bench Press Calculator"
  h1="Bench Press One Rep Max (1RM) Calculator"
  lead="Estimate your bench press max from a set you have already done — no max attempt needed. Enter the weight and the clean reps you completed."
  exercise="bench-press"
  lift="Bench press"
  example={{ weight: 185, reps: 5, unit: 'lb' }}
  related={[LINKS.increase, LINKS.percent, LINKS.trainingMax, LINKS.ohp]}
  sources={['mayhew', 'lesuer', 'reynolds', 'hprc']}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="bench-estimate-heading">
    <h2 id="bench-estimate-heading">How to estimate your bench press max</h2>
    <p class="answer">
      Bench a weight you can lift for 3–6 clean reps, stop when the next rep would be a grind or
      a miss, and enter the weight and reps above. The calculator returns six formula estimates and
      their average — for example, 185 lb × 5 estimates a bench max of roughly 208–220 lb.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bench press is the lift most 1RM equations were built around. Mayhew&rsquo;s formula was
      fitted to bench-press data from college men and women, and later studies that compared
      equations against tested maxes almost always included the bench (LeSuer et al., 1997;
      Reynolds et al., 2006). That makes it the lift where formula estimates have been checked most.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="bench-rep-heading">
    <h2 id="bench-rep-heading">What counts as a clean bench rep</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Touch the chest every rep.</strong> Stopping a few inches short shortens the range of motion and inflates the estimate.</li>
      <li><strong>No bouncing.</strong> Bouncing the bar off the chest adds momentum the formulas do not account for. A brief touch is fine; a pause is stricter still.</li>
      <li><strong>Hips stay on the bench.</strong> Lifting the hips changes the pressing angle and makes reps easier than they should be.</li>
      <li><strong>Same grip and setup as your heavy sets.</strong> A narrower or wider grip than usual will give a different number.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you compete or test with a paused bench, estimate from paused reps too. Touch-and-go
      reps are usually stronger than paused ones, so an estimate from touch-and-go sets can read
      high for a paused max.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="bench-safety-heading">
    <h2 id="bench-safety-heading">Bench press safety for heavy sets</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use a spotter who knows when to take the bar, or bench inside a rack with safety arms set just below chest height.</li>
      <li>Use a full grip with the thumb wrapped around the bar — a thumbless grip lets the bar roll out of your hands.</li>
      <li>For a true max attempt, follow a structured protocol: see <a href="/guides/how-to-test-1rm/">how to test your 1RM safely</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="bench-use-heading">
    <h2 id="bench-use-heading">Using your bench estimate in training</h2>
    <p>
      Bench strength usually moves in small steps, so percentages matter. A 5% change on a 225 lb
      bench is only about 11 lb. The <a href="/1rm-percentage-calculator/">percentage calculator</a>
      rounds every working weight <em>down</em> to a loadable 5 lb or 2.5 kg step, and the
      plate loader in your results shows exactly what goes on each side.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="bench-faq-heading">
    <h2 id="bench-faq-heading">Bench press max: common questions</h2>
    <h3>How do I calculate my bench max by hand?</h3>
    <p>
      Use the Epley formula: <strong>1RM = weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30)</strong>. For 185 lb × 5:
      185 × (1 + 5 ÷ 30) = <strong>215.8 lb</strong>. The calculator runs five more published
      formulas and shows the range between them.
    </p>
    <h3>Is a max estimated from 10 reps accurate?</h3>
    <p>
      Less accurate than one from a shorter set. In a study using the chest press and leg press, a
      5-rep max predicted the 1RM more closely than a 10- or 20-rep max (Reynolds et al., 2006). For
      the best estimate, use a hard set of 3–6 reps.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I use a dumbbell or Smith machine set?</h3>
    <p>
      Only to estimate a max for that same lift. The formulas do not know what equipment you used,
      so a dumbbell or Smith machine set predicts a dumbbell or Smith machine max, not a barbell bench.
    </p>
    <h3>How often should I update my bench max?</h3>
    <p>
      Whenever you complete a new hard set in the 3–6 rep range. For many lifters that is every few
      weeks, or at the end of each training block.
    </p>
  </section>
</ExerciseLayout>
